        "My father called me 'the future of this family' more times than he ever called me his son. I noticed the difference by the time I was seven, sitting through my first lesson in what he called 'necessary exposure' — a term I understood, even at seven, was a euphemism for something he didn't want to name directly to a child, even his own. He took me to the west wing basement that year, the first of many such trips, and made me watch while two of his men extracted information from a man who had, apparently, stolen from the organization. I didn't understand, at seven, what I was watching. I understood only that my father's hand on my shoulder throughout the entire hour was not comfort. It was evaluation. He was watching my face the whole time, not the man in the chair, checking for weakness the way you'd check a foundation for cracks before building anything heavier on top of it.",
